# Operational Rules for Cline

## 1. API RATE LIMIT & COOLING & ERROR HANDLING

- In case of 429 (Resource Exhausted) or 503 (Service Unavailable) errors, DO NOT engage in aggressive or infinite retry loops[cite: 1].
- Group file reads to minimize consecutive API calls[cite: 1].
- Maintain a 2 to 3-second gap between autonomous actions to avoid saturating the Requests Per Minute (RPM) limit[cite: 1].

### Automated Error Handling (429 / 503)

- If an API response returns 429 or 503:
  1. Stop execution immediately after a maximum of 1 retry attempt. Do NOT lock the user interface with auto-retry loops.
  2. Prompt the user clearly: "Se detectó saturación o indisponibilidad en el modelo actual (Error 503/429). Por favor, cambia temporalmente el modelo en la configuración de Cline o reintenta en unos instantes."[cite: 1]
  3. Pause operations until the user changes the model or confirms retrying[cite: 1].

## 2. MODULAR EXECUTION & ATOMIC SCOPE

- **Single Task Focus:** Execute changes in small, atomic steps. Never attempt multi-repository consolidations or large structural refactors in a single pass.
- **Incremental Progress:** Modify or migrate ONE module/file at a time, verify that it compiles without errors, and await user confirmation before proceeding to the next task.

## 3. TERMINAL SYNTAX (POWERSHELL)

- This environment uses Windows PowerShell 5.1[cite: 1].
- NEVER use the `&&` operator to chain commands. Use `;` or separate commands instead[cite: 1].

## 4. REPOSITORY SECURITY & FROZEN FILES

- **PROTECTED FILES:** DO NOT modify `src/data/cities.json` or edit map/route URLs under any circumstances unless explicitly commanded in the immediate prompt.
- Keep changes small, clean, and iterative[cite: 1].
- Do not modify or delete legacy files or backup databases[cite: 1].
- Always confirm the active Git branch before editing code[cite: 1].